  "Comments": "Vapor pressure to be checked: the open sources don't give a sterol curve, so the squalene curve of Zafar and Chickos (2019) is scaled by 0.724 to the DSC boiling point of beta-tocopherol, 552.73 K at 1.1 kPa (Damaceno et al. 2014), by analogy, within 2.7 %. Normal boiling point 723.8 K from that curve. Tc, Pc and the ideal gas formation enthalpy and Gibbs energy by Joback (1987) from that boiling point, acentric factor by Lee-Kesler. Heat of vaporization from the slope of the vapor pressure curve (100.1 kJ/mol at 500 K), entered at the normal boiling point with Watson's exponent. Heat capacities, transport properties and surface tension of Squalane (ChemSep), per unit mass. Liquid density 960 kg/m3 at 20 C (to be checked). UNIFAC groups approximate.",
